# CI/CD

## What I am learning

How software moves from a Git commit to a running application: automated tests, build pipelines, artifacts, environments, deployment strategies, secrets, rollback, and pipeline security.

I already have practical experience with Jenkins. This topic uses it as a starting point and connects it to the concepts underneath, so that the knowledge transfers to any CI/CD tool.

## Why it matters

Manual builds and deployments are slow, inconsistent, and risky. A good pipeline makes every change pass the same quality gates, produces the same artifact every time, and makes deployment and rollback routine instead of stressful. The pipeline is also a high-value attack target, so its security matters.

> **Goal:** understand the complete path from Git commit to running application.

---

## Learning objectives

By the end of this topic I can:

- Explain continuous integration, continuous delivery, and continuous deployment, and how they differ
- Design a pipeline with clear stages and quality gates
- Run automated tests, linting, and security checks on every change
- Build a container image in the pipeline and tag it traceably
- Store and version artifacts in a registry
- Manage separate environments (development, staging, production) and their configuration
- Handle secrets safely in a pipeline
- Explain deployment strategies (recreate, rolling, blue/green, canary) and their trade-offs
- Roll back a bad release quickly
- Explain pipeline security risks and mitigations
- Express the same pipeline in two different tools to separate concepts from syntax

---

## Weekly progression

| Week | Focus |
|---|---|
| 1 | Concepts: CI versus CD, pipeline stages, quality gates; map my existing Jenkins knowledge to these concepts |
| 2 | Jenkins pipeline as code (`Jenkinsfile`): checkout, test, build, with tests required to pass |
| 3 | Docker in the pipeline: build, tag, scan, and push an image to a registry |
| 4 | Git-based CI: rebuild the same pipeline in a hosted tool (for example GitHub Actions) and compare |
| 5 | Environments, secrets, and configuration management |
| 6 | Deployment strategies and rollback |
| 7 | Pipeline security and mini-project |
| 8 | Review, and connect the pipeline to the main project |

This topic delivers Stage 4 of the main project: Git, then CI pipeline, tests, build, container image, and deployment.

