## Checklist

- [ ] Explain CI, continuous delivery, and continuous deployment
- [ ] Explain what a quality gate is and give examples
- [ ] Write a pipeline as code in Jenkins
- [ ] Run unit and integration tests in the pipeline and fail the build on failure
- [ ] Add linting and a dependency vulnerability scan
- [ ] Build a Docker image in the pipeline
- [ ] Tag images with the commit hash instead of only `latest`
- [ ] Push an image to a registry
- [ ] Rebuild the pipeline in a second tool and compare the two
- [ ] Separate configuration per environment
- [ ] Store secrets in the CI secret store, never in the repository or in logs
- [ ] Explain rolling, blue/green, and canary deployments
- [ ] Roll back a release
- [ ] Explain why builds should be reproducible
- [ ] Explain pipeline security risks (leaked secrets, untrusted pull requests, compromised dependencies)
- [ ] Trace a commit all the way to the running application

A box is ticked only when I can do or explain the task without relying entirely on a tutorial.

---

## The pipeline I am building

```text
Git push
   ↓
Checkout
   ↓
Lint + unit tests
   ↓
Build
   ↓
Integration tests
   ↓
Security scan (dependencies, image)
   ↓
Build and tag container image (commit hash)
   ↓
Push to registry
   ↓
Deploy to staging
   ↓
Smoke test
   ↓
Deploy to production (approval or automatic)
   ↓
Health check, with automatic rollback on failure
```

---

## Exercises

- [ ] Write a `Jenkinsfile` with stages for checkout, test, and build
- [ ] Make the pipeline fail when one unit test fails, then fix it
- [ ] Cache dependencies and measure the build-time difference
- [ ] Tag an image with the short commit hash and find which commit produced a running container
- [ ] Rebuild the same pipeline in a second CI tool and write down what changed and what stayed the same
- [ ] Add a secret to the CI secret store and use it without printing it
- [ ] Add a manual approval step before production

## Labs

Each lab ends with a written note: symptom, evidence, hypothesis, root cause, fix, lesson.

- [ ] **Works locally, fails in CI:** find the environment difference (version, missing variable, file path)
- [ ] **Flaky pipeline:** a test or step fails intermittently, find the cause rather than re-running
- [ ] **Leaked secret in logs:** a secret appears in build output, find it, fix it, and explain why rotating it matters
- [ ] **Bad release:** a deployment passes the pipeline but breaks production, detect it with a health check and roll back
- [ ] **Untrusted pull request:** show how a pipeline can be abused by code from a fork and how to restrict it
- [ ] **Slow pipeline:** find the slowest stage and speed it up with evidence

## Mini-project: End-to-end pipeline for the main project

A pipeline for the main project that includes:

- Automated lint, unit tests, and integration tests
- A dependency and image vulnerability scan
- A container image tagged with the commit hash and pushed to a registry
- Deployment to a staging environment, then production
- A smoke test and health check after each deployment
- A documented, tested rollback procedure
- Secrets handled only through the CI secret store
- The pipeline defined as code in the repository
- A README with a diagram of the full flow, from commit to running application
- An ADR for the CI tool and deployment strategy

